            <ArticleTitle>The pars defect as a pain source. A histologic study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1761-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Tissue from the pars defects of six adult patients with symptomatic spondylolysis and spondylolisthesis was obtained at surgery. A histologic study was conducted to identify and characterize neural elements in this tissue.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine if nociceptive nerve endings were present within the pars defect of patients with symptomatic spondylolysis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The origin of back pain in patients with spondylolysis remains uncertain. The defect in the pars interarticularis has been implicated as a possible pain source.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The soft tissue from the pars defect was obtained at surgery. A modified gold chloride stain was used to prepare the tissue for histologic examination. Tissue blocks were sectioned and studied under light microscopy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Neural elements were found in all specimens examined. Free nerve endings believed to have nociceptive function were identified in all specimens. The density of neural elements varied between specimens.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The finding of neural elements, including free nerve endings within the pars defect tissue, suggests that the pars defect may be a source of back pain in some patients with symptomatic spondylolysis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
